<section class="section" id="generation">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="grid grid-2" data-reveal>
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Generation</p>
        <h2>Synthetic rows from a spec, or from the shape of real data</h2>
        <p class="muted">Twelve generation types and 34 domain providers (names, addresses, SSN, NPI, MRN, NDC, IBAN, PAN, CUSIP, ICD-10 and more). Run <code>decoy fit</code> on a real table to capture histograms, quantiles, and top-k values, then sample from that snapshot instead of inventing a distribution.</p>
        <p class="muted">Marginal differential privacy is available for generated data: per-column mechanisms from OpenDP with a single (epsilon, delta) budget. It covers the column types you declare and does not extend to relationships between columns or to masked output. <a href="/docs/concepts/differential-privacy">What it does and does not cover</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <ul class="stack" style="list-style: none; padding: 0; color: var(--fg-muted); font-size: var(--text-sm)">
          <li><strong class="tag-safe">categorical, distribution, sequence</strong> for shape</li>
          <li><strong class="tag-safe">faker, formula, derived, derived_aggregate</strong> for values</li>
          <li><strong class="tag-safe">reference, group_key, grouped_series</strong> for child tables that point at generated parents</li>
          <li><strong class="tag-safe">statistical, windowed_date</strong> for fitted snapshots and time windows</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="generation-demo" data-reveal>
      <h3>One YAML, a whole referentially intact set</h3>
      <p class="muted">No input data. A child column of <code>type: reference</code> draws its key from a generated parent, so every <code>orders.customer_id</code> is a real <code>customers.customer_id</code>. One document builds both tables, with the foreign key enforced.</p>
      <div class="terminal">
        <div class="terminal-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>generate.yaml</span></div>
<pre><span class="t-line dim">version: 1</span>
<span class="t-line dim">global_settings: {seed: 42}</span>
<span class="t-line dim">sources: {}</span>
<span class="t-line">&nbsp;</span>
<span class="t-line">tables:</span>
<span class="t-line">  - name: <span class="safe">customers</span>          <span class="dim"># parent</span></span>
<span class="t-line">    row_count: 1000</span>
<span class="t-line">    generate_columns:</span>
<span class="t-line">      - {name: customer_id, type: sequence, start: 100000}</span>
<span class="t-line">      - {name: first_name, type: faker, faker_type: first_name}</span>
<span class="t-line">      - {name: segment, type: categorical, categories: [retail, sme, enterprise]}</span>
<span class="t-line">  - name: <span class="safe">orders</span>             <span class="dim"># child, points at customers</span></span>
<span class="t-line">    row_count: 5000</span>
<span class="t-line">    generate_columns:</span>
<span class="t-line">      - {name: order_id, type: sequence, start: 1}</span>
<span class="t-line">      - name: customer_id      <span class="dim"># the foreign key</span></span>
<span class="t-line">        <span class="safe">type: reference</span></span>
<span class="t-line">        <span class="safe">reference_table: customers</span></span>
<span class="t-line">        <span class="safe">reference_column: customer_id</span></span>
<span class="t-line">      - {name: status, type: categorical, categories: [placed, shipped, returned]}</span>
<span class="t-line">&nbsp;</span>
<span class="t-line dim">targets:</span>
<span class="t-line dim">  customers: {type: file, format: csv, path: ./out/customers.csv}</span>
<span class="t-line dim">  orders:    {type: file, format: csv, path: ./out/orders.csv}</span></pre>
      </div>
      <p class="join-note">{% icon "link" %}<span><strong>Referentially complete.</strong> 1,000 customers and 5,000 orders, every order tied to a generated customer. No orphans, no real data in, and you can subset or re-run it deterministically from the same seed.</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="subsetting">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="grid grid-2" data-reveal>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Subsetting</p>
        <h3>A small, referentially intact slice</h3>
        <p>Start from a filter on one table and walk the declared relationships to a fixpoint under a fan-out budget. Composite and self-referencing keys are supported; nulls pass through; orphan rows follow the policy you set: preserve, remap, warn, or fail. Parquet in, Parquet out.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card" id="detection">
        <p class="eyebrow">Detection</p>
        <h3>The scan finds it, you decide</h3>
        <p><code>decoy scan</code> profiles every column with 25 value detectors and tags each finding with a domain: identity, financial, health, contact, location. After a run, <code>decoy report integrity</code> checks the output for residual PII and FK parity and returns exit code 4 on findings. An optional spaCy model adds person and location detection in free text.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="disguises">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
      <p class="eyebrow">Disguises</p>
      <h2>Policy templates for the frameworks you answer to</h2>
      <p>A Disguise is a dated, versioned bundle of column-pattern-to-strategy rules. Ten ship with the engine: HIPAA Safe Harbor (21 rules), PCI, GDPR, GLBA, CCPA, CPNI, FERPA, SOX, property and casualty insurance, and a default. They are templates that encode a reading of each framework, not certifications.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cluster">
      <span class="pill pill--accent">hipaa</span><span class="pill pill--accent">pci</span><span class="pill pill--accent">gdpr</span><span class="pill pill--accent">glba</span><span class="pill pill--accent">ccpa</span><span class="pill pill--accent">cpni</span><span class="pill pill--accent">ferpa</span><span class="pill pill--accent">sox</span><span class="pill pill--accent">pc</span><span class="pill pill--accent">default</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="determinism">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="grid grid-2" data-reveal>
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Determinism and joins</p>
        <h2>Same secret, same input, same output. Everywhere.</h2>
        <p class="muted">Keyed strategies derive their output with HKDF-SHA256 from a mask secret you supply (at least 32 bytes, from an environment variable or a file) and the source value. Tables that share a namespace mask the same value the same way, so joins between them survive and output is byte-stable across runs.</p>
        <p class="muted">Format-preserving encryption uses NIST SP 800-38G FF1 with AES-256 and refuses domains under one million values. FPE columns reverse from the config alone; one-way columns can be made reversible by writing an encrypted token vault.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="terminal">
        <div class="terminal-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>pipeline.yaml</span></div>
<pre>{% snippet "pipeline.yaml" %}</pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="ci">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
      <p class="eyebrow">CI</p>
      <h2>Made to run unattended</h2>
      <p><code>--json</code> writes results to stdout and progress to stderr. <code>--quiet</code> leaves only the exit code. <code>--evidence-out</code> records what ran without recording a single raw value, and <code>decoy report verify</code> checks it later.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Exit code</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Typical cause</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>OK</td><td>Run completed, checks passed</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>Usage</td><td>Bad flags or arguments</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>3</code></td><td>Runtime</td><td>Unreadable input, strategy error, I/O failure</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>4</code></td><td>Findings</td><td>Residual PII, FK parity or policy violations from <code>report integrity</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>5</code></td><td>Capacity</td><td><code>preflight</code> estimates the job cannot fit on this host</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

